; Every position at which generated code NAMES the type of a member, exercised on ONE alias each, so
; a re-resolution at ANY single emission path shows up as a snapshot diff on a fixture whose whole
; purpose is the spelling rule (`docs/docs/output_format.mdx` § "Type spelling at member positions").
;
; The rule is that a type-DECLARATION position spells the member's type AS DECLARED, keeping the
; outermost alias ident. Exactly one function spells member types
; (`ConceptualRustType::for_rust_member_ct`) and it already keeps the alias, so every resolved
; spelling in the output comes from a caller that resolved for STRUCTURAL DISPATCH and then reused
; the dispatch-normalized value as a NAMING input — which is invisible at any one site, and is how
; the emission paths drifted from each other. This fixture makes the paths visible together.
;
; Positions covered, and what to look for under `--preserve-encodings`:
;   * data-struct field, constructor param, getter/setter        -> `pid: PolicyId`
;   * a named collection rule's own alias target                 -> `pub type Nemap = OrderedHashMap<Epoch, String>`
;   * encoding sidecar INDEX KEY, declared map field             -> `key_1_key_encodings: BTreeMap<Epoch, ..>`
;     (this one MUST be the data field's key type: serialize looks the sidecar up with a key
;     borrowed straight out of the data map, so the two are one type expressed twice)
;   * encoding sidecar, open-struct rest row, depth 1            -> `rest_key_encodings: BTreeMap<Epoch, ..>`
;   * encoding sidecar, open-struct rest row, depth 2            -> the nested `BTreeMap<PolicyId, ..>`
;     inside the rest VALUE's tuple. Depth 1 was declared while depth 2 was resolved INSIDE ONE TYPE
;     EXPRESSION, which is the defect in its purest form and the reason this fixture spans depths.
;   * encoding sidecar, rest ARRAY tail element                  -> `rest_elem_encodings: Vec<(.., BTreeMap<PolicyId, ..>, ..)>`
;   * container descent (Array / Optional / nested Map inners)    -> `arr`, `opt`, `deep`
;
; Also covered, and deliberately still RESOLVED here: the member deserialize CALL TARGETS
; (`Int::deserialize` for `dc`, `Credential::deserialize` for `sc`). Those are a different position
; class, out of scope for the declared-spelling change at type declarations; this fixture's committed
; snapshot is the diff surface for whenever that class is taken on, so the positions are exercised
; now rather than added later.
;
; Shape constraint worth keeping: no `@duplicates preserve` table sharing a key/value shape with a
; rest row in this file. That combination trips a pre-existing wasm wrapper-name collision
; (`MapEpochToText` claimed by both the pair-map twin and the rest row) unrelated to spelling.

policy_id = bytes
epoch = uint
delta_coin = int
label = text

credential = [idx: uint, hash: policy_id]
stake_credential = credential

nemap = { * epoch => label }

; data field / ctor param / accessor, plus the two deserialize call targets
holder = [
  pid: policy_id,
  dc: delta_coin,
  sc: stake_credential,
  m: nemap,
]

; declared map field: the encoding sidecar's index key must be the field's DECLARED key type
keyed = { 1: { * epoch => label } }

; container descent — an alias under Array, under Optional, and as the key of a nested Map
nested = [
  arr: [* policy_id],
  opt: policy_id / null,
  deep: { * epoch => { * policy_id => label } },
]

; open struct-map rest row: depth 1 is the key domain, depth 2 the container inside the rest VALUE
open_holder = {
  1: uint,
  * epoch => { * policy_id => label },
}

; open array rest tail: the element is a container whose key is an alias
open_tail = [uint, * { * policy_id => label }]
